Thank you.

1033
00:40:17,184 --> 00:40:18,213
- Congratulations. - Enjoy the food.

1034
00:40:18,214 --> 00:40:19,953
- We'll go and say hello soon. - Okay.

1035
00:40:19,954 --> 00:40:21,123
- Jang Woo, see you soon. - Jang Woo put...

1036
00:40:21,124 --> 00:40:22,684
a lot of care into the food.

1037
00:40:22,984 --> 00:40:25,493
- It was tasty. - It was really tasty.

1038
00:40:25,494 --> 00:40:26,924
- He didn't disappoint. - Jang Woo didn't disappoint.

1039
00:40:27,624 --> 00:40:29,794
Groom and bride don't even have time to eat.

1040
00:40:32,064 --> 00:40:33,664
At what age do you want to get married?

1041
00:40:34,234 --> 00:40:37,033
I got 4 readings in the 4 Pillars of Destiny.

1042
00:40:37,034 --> 00:40:39,104
They told me to get married at the age of 35 or 36.

1043
00:40:39,604 --> 00:40:40,604
So I want to get married in five years.

1044
00:40:41,704 --> 00:40:42,904
That's the plan.

1045
00:40:42,974 --> 00:40:44,244
At the age of 35?

1046
00:40:44,614 --> 00:40:45,614
Why?

1047
00:40:45,615 --> 00:40:47,044
(Shaking his head)

1048
00:40:47,314 --> 00:40:49,444
- Don't believe in such things. - Did you get married at that age?

1049
00:40:49,744 --> 00:40:51,513
At what age do you want to get married?

1050
00:40:51,514 --> 00:40:52,984
I don't want to get married.

1051
00:40:54,454 --> 00:40:55,724
I don't want to get married.

1052
00:40:56,124 --> 00:40:57,354
Have you always wanted to stay celibate?

1053
00:40:57,524 --> 00:40:59,393
- I think I'm a bit like that. - I see.

1054
00:40:59,394 --> 00:41:01,094
- Really? - He's a celibate.

1055
00:41:01,194 --> 00:41:03,564
I never feel jealous when I watch people's weddings.

1056
00:41:03,994 --> 00:41:05,333
If I feel jealous,

1057
00:41:05,334 --> 00:41:06,863
- Right. - I'll want to get married.

1058
00:41:06,864 --> 00:41:09,434
But I never think, "I want to get married someday."

1059
00:41:09,864 --> 00:41:11,574
What's under the gochujang sole?

1060
00:41:11,904 --> 00:41:12,933
It's saffron risotto.

1061
00:41:12,934 --> 00:41:13,943
Saffron risotto.

1062
00:41:13,944 --> 00:41:15,703
That dish was tasty.

1063
00:41:15,704 --> 00:41:18,043
It's like a dish...

1064
00:41:18,044 --> 00:41:19,473
a foreign chef has made...

1065
00:41:19,474 --> 00:41:20,884
- for Koreans. - That's right.

1066
00:41:22,014 --> 00:41:23,853
(She takes a bite of the steak.)

1067
00:41:23,854 --> 00:41:26,154
- The steak was nice and tender. - It was tender.

1068
00:41:26,714 --> 00:41:28,424
(Eating)

1069
00:41:30,294 --> 00:41:31,794
The abalone is tasty.

1070
00:41:31,894 --> 00:41:32,994
It's so tasty.

1071
00:41:34,494 --> 00:41:36,494
- They're here. - They're here.

1072
00:41:37,864 --> 00:41:39,963
The reception. Their outfits are so pretty.

1073
00:41:39,964 --> 00:41:41,404
- He looks cool. - They're pretty.

1074
00:41:43,304 --> 00:41:45,503
- I was so thankful... - Congratulations.

1075
00:41:45,504 --> 00:41:46,973
since he came to our table as soon as the reception began.

1076
00:41:46,974 --> 00:41:48,844
- I know. - I was so thankful.

1077
00:41:49,074 --> 00:41:51,413
Why did you wear white?

1078
00:41:51,414 --> 00:41:52,944
- What? - Where did you get the courage?

1079
00:41:53,414 --> 00:41:54,444
What?

1080
00:41:55,644 --> 00:41:57,584
- It's my wedding. - You're like mashed potato.

1081
00:41:58,984 --> 00:42:00,723
- Jang Woo seemed like an adult. - Thank you for coming.

1082
00:42:00,724 --> 00:42:01,854
He's an adult.

1083
00:42:01,954 --> 00:42:04,094
- Today is Jang Woo's last day. - You're right.

1084
00:42:04,754 --> 00:42:06,053
He'll graduate from I Live Alone.

1085
00:42:06,054 --> 00:42:07,493
- Congratulations. - We'll live well.

1086
00:42:07,494 --> 00:42:09,163
- Thank you. - Congratulations.

1087
00:42:09,164 --> 00:42:10,193
Live well.

1088
00:42:10,194 --> 00:42:11,893
(The Rainbow Club members congratulate him sincerely.)

1089
00:42:11,894 --> 00:42:13,433
- Thank you. - Congratulations.

1090
00:42:13,434 --> 00:42:14,734
- Congratulations. - Mr. Groom.

1091
00:42:15,264 --> 00:42:16,663
- I'll get going now. - Okay.

1092
00:42:16,664 --> 00:42:18,173
- I'll get going now. - See you tomorrow.

1093
00:42:18,174 --> 00:42:19,404
See you tomorrow. I'll get going now.

1094
00:42:19,574 --> 00:42:20,874
I'll get going now, Kang Yong.

1095
00:42:21,044 --> 00:42:22,574
- Well done, Hyun Moo. - That's right. Well done.

1096
00:42:22,774 --> 00:42:24,474
Congratulations on your perfect debut as a host.

1097
00:42:25,444 --> 00:42:26,514
I'll get going now.

1098
00:42:26,774 --> 00:42:27,914
Thank you for your hard work.

1099
00:42:28,284 --> 00:42:31,314
(The officiant leaves the wedding venue.)

1100
00:42:31,954 --> 00:42:35,254
When you tuned around and headed out,

1101
00:42:35,654 --> 00:42:37,094
you looked so forlorn.

1102
00:42:37,424 --> 00:42:38,724
You looked so lonely.

1103
00:42:47,404 --> 00:42:48,504
I'm tired.

1104
00:42:51,204 --> 00:42:53,403
- Look at him. - Oh, no.

1105
00:42:53,404 --> 00:42:54,674
I don't think I can watch this.

1106
00:42:54,874 --> 00:42:56,343
What? Why do I look so depressed?

1107
00:42:56,344 --> 00:42:58,474
He brought flowers.

1108
00:43:04,114 --> 00:43:06,414
(Haggard)

1109
00:43:09,154 --> 00:43:10,753
Did he come back so late?

1110
00:43:10,754 --> 00:43:12,954
The wedding began at 6pm.

1111
00:43:14,724 --> 00:43:16,424
- My goodness. - My goodness.

1112
00:43:17,494 --> 00:43:18,693
My goodness.

1113
00:43:18,694 --> 00:43:19,994
It's so quiet.

1114
00:43:24,574 --> 00:43:25,703
It was already on the internet...

1115
00:43:25,704 --> 00:43:27,074
because everyone had filmed it.

1116
00:43:28,474 --> 00:43:30,844
(Fidgeting)

1117
00:43:31,844 --> 00:43:33,344
(Like)

1118
00:43:33,944 --> 00:43:35,183
Do you know the person who posted it?

1119
00:43:35,184 --> 00:43:36,184
No.

1120
00:43:38,414 --> 00:43:39,654
I was tagged.

1121
00:43:40,884 --> 00:43:42,123
(The short-form videos he's in)

1122
00:43:42,124 --> 00:43:43,253
(Like)

1123
00:43:43,254 --> 00:43:44,394
He likes everything.

1124
00:43:45,524 --> 00:43:47,164
He likes all the posts about him.

1125
00:43:48,494 --> 00:43:51,134
(He regains energy by looking at himself on social media.)

1126
00:43:53,834 --> 00:43:55,904
(Hmph)

1127
00:43:59,134 --> 00:44:00,904
(Getting up)

1128
00:44:03,644 --> 00:44:07,314
(He puts the flowers from the wedding in a vase.)

1129
00:44:09,884 --> 00:44:11,354
I'm so exhausted.

1130
00:44:12,014 --> 00:44:13,684
My energy is drained.

1131
00:44:14,384 --> 00:44:16,954
(He comes back to the sofa with flowers.)

1132
00:44:17,294 --> 00:44:19,293
It's nice that there are flowers.

1133
00:44:19,294 --> 00:44:21,194
I know. They're nice to look at.

1134
00:44:22,794 --> 00:44:24,934
- Is that the wedding message? - Yes. It's what he wrote earlier.

1135
00:44:26,434 --> 00:44:28,333
(He unfolds the wedding message and pressed it with a book.)

1136
00:44:28,334 --> 00:44:31,733
- No way. - Will he take a photo?

1137
00:44:31,734 --> 00:44:33,273
(He takes a photo to show that he has officiated a wedding.)

1138
00:44:33,274 --> 00:44:35,544
I wondered why he brought back the flowers.

1139
00:44:40,814 --> 00:44:41,844
The new groom.

1140
00:44:42,344 --> 00:44:44,654
- Hyun Moo. - What? Are you home already?

1141
00:44:44,714 --> 00:44:47,224
No. We cleaned up...

1142
00:44:47,584 --> 00:44:49,154
and came back to the waiting room.

1143
00:44:49,554 --> 00:44:50,993
Thank you so much, Hyun Moo.

1144
00:44:50,994 --> 00:44:54,624
Don't mention it. You gave me a good opportunity.

1145
00:44:55,394 --> 00:44:57,664
When you looked at me earlier,

1146
00:44:58,494 --> 00:45:00,334
I was moved.

1147
00:45:01,564 --> 00:45:03,664
- Right? - I had planned...

1148
00:45:04,204 --> 00:45:06,133
to deliver the message...

1149
00:45:06,134 --> 00:45:07,703
- Okay. - while looking at you guys.

1150
00:45:07,704 --> 00:45:10,643
But your eyes became a bit moist.

1151
00:45:10,644 --> 00:45:11,713
He was right.

1152
00:45:11,714 --> 00:45:12,774
(Moist)

1153
00:45:12,944 --> 00:45:16,314
So I looked at the space between you and Hye Won...

1154
00:45:16,714 --> 00:45:18,354
to avoid meeting your eye.

1155
00:45:18,754 --> 00:45:19,783
Right.

1156
00:45:19,784 --> 00:45:22,254
It was so touching. Thank you.

1157
00:45:22,754 --> 00:45:24,423
We'll live well, Hyun Moo.

1158
00:45:24,424 --> 00:45:25,653
Okay, Jang Woo.

1159
00:45:25,654 --> 00:45:27,364
You must have many people to call. You should hang up now.

1160
00:45:27,694 --> 00:45:29,663
Okay, Hyun Moo. Thank you.

1161
00:45:29,664 --> 00:45:30,734
Okay.

1162
00:45:33,734 --> 00:45:34,834
My goodness.

1163
00:45:35,064 --> 00:45:36,704
- My goodness. - My goodness.

1164
00:45:36,964 --> 00:45:38,533
Why is everyone sighing?

1165
00:45:38,534 --> 00:45:40,074
It isn't like that.

1166
00:45:40,174 --> 00:45:41,744
How did you feel?

1167
00:45:42,544 --> 00:45:43,544
I was jealous.

1168
00:45:43,545 --> 00:45:45,314
- Really? - I was jealous of Jang Woo.

1169
00:45:45,514 --> 00:45:47,313
Usually, when I host a wedding,

1170
00:45:47,314 --> 00:45:49,313
I never think, "I feel lonely."

1171
00:45:49,314 --> 00:45:50,914
"I want to be like them."

1172
00:45:51,184 --> 00:45:53,214
But they looked so happy.

1173
00:45:53,325 --> 00:45:55,183
I felt jealous for the first time in a long while...

1174
00:45:55,184 --> 00:45:56,724
after the wedding.

1175
00:45:58,554 --> 00:45:59,594
I'm a bit hungry.

1176
00:46:02,394 --> 00:46:03,894
Will you eat ramyeon?

1177
00:46:04,234 --> 00:46:05,293
Of course.

1178
00:46:05,294 --> 00:46:06,864
A steak wasn't enough to fill me up.

1179
00:46:08,804 --> 00:46:10,974
(Adding the seasoning)

1180
00:46:12,834 --> 00:46:15,244
In the Han River ramyeon style, I kept the lid open.

1181
00:46:16,904 --> 00:46:19,044
Live happily.

1182
00:46:20,884 --> 00:46:21,884
(Sniffing)

1183
00:46:25,054 --> 00:46:26,153
What are those?

1184
00:46:26,154 --> 00:46:28,283
Those are wild-harvested pine mushrooms.

1185
00:46:28,284 --> 00:46:29,383
My goodness.

1186
00:46:29,384 --> 00:46:30,593
I always put them in ramyeon.

1187
00:46:30,594 --> 00:46:31,853
- Pine mushrooms in ramyeon? - What a waste.

1188
00:46:31,854 --> 00:46:33,294
Do you put them in ramyeon?

1189
00:46:34,594 --> 00:46:36,464
I don't have time to cook.

1190
00:46:41,404 --> 00:46:42,504
How precious.

1191
00:46:42,734 --> 00:46:44,304
Are they different from the mushroom you get at a barbecue place?

1192
00:46:44,404 --> 00:46:45,833
- They're totally different. - They're totally different.

1193
00:46:45,834 --> 00:46:47,504
- They're very expensive. - They're very expensive.

1194
00:46:47,844 --> 00:46:48,904
I see.

1195
00:46:51,774 --> 00:46:52,814
It's tasty.

1196
00:46:56,184 --> 00:46:57,514
(Is it cooked?)

1197
00:46:58,714 --> 00:46:59,984
It looks tasty.

1198
00:47:02,284 --> 00:47:03,483
With kimchi...

1199
00:47:03,484 --> 00:47:05,593
(He adds kimchi and Woo Il's dried laver.)

1200
00:47:05,594 --> 00:47:07,093
- Woo Il's dried laver. - It's tasty.

1201
00:47:07,094 --> 00:47:08,124
It's tasty.

1202
00:47:08,694 --> 00:47:10,494
I'll add more saltiness to salty food.

1203
00:47:12,694 --> 00:47:14,704
(Stirring)

1204
00:47:15,934 --> 00:47:17,874
(Slurping)

1205
00:47:19,974 --> 00:47:20,974
Do you eat standing up?

1206
00:47:20,975 --> 00:47:22,104
I eat standing up these days.

1207
00:47:23,904 --> 00:47:26,044
(He puts a piece of kimchi on the top.)

1208
00:47:27,984 --> 00:47:30,684
(He inhales the food hurriedly.)

1209
00:47:32,954 --> 00:47:34,924
That and Woo Il's dried laver.

1210
00:47:35,024 --> 00:47:36,383
The dried laver is very tasty.

1211
00:47:36,384 --> 00:47:38,554
- That's right. It's tasty. - The dried laver is tasty.

1212
00:47:39,624 --> 00:47:41,394
(Crunchy)

1213
00:47:43,464 --> 00:47:45,164
I love...

1214
00:47:45,564 --> 00:47:47,034
eating salty food with salty food.

1215
00:47:47,194 --> 00:47:48,304
It's a salty meal.

1216
00:47:49,804 --> 00:47:51,534
Why is ramyeon so tasty?

1217
00:47:53,974 --> 00:47:55,303
(He fills his body with sodium.)

1218
00:47:55,304 --> 00:47:57,274
My goodness. Oh, no.

1219
00:47:57,814 --> 00:47:59,714
- Oh, no. - He choked on his food.

1220
00:48:00,884 --> 00:48:03,313
(Hyun Moo)

1221
00:48:03,314 --> 00:48:04,913
- My goodness. - I thought he was eating hurriedly.

1222
00:48:04,914 --> 00:48:06,184
He should've sat down.

1223
00:48:07,624 --> 00:48:08,653
It's gone already.

1224
00:48:08,654 --> 00:48:10,754
(He slurps the noodles as he remains standing up.)

1225
00:48:13,094 --> 00:48:15,323
Even after a full-course meal at the wedding venue,

1226
00:48:15,324 --> 00:48:17,534
I had room for ramyeon.

1227
00:48:21,134 --> 00:48:22,933
(He drinks the soup from the pot.)

1228
00:48:22,934 --> 00:48:24,604
It had the pine mushroom aroma.

1229
00:48:26,334 --> 00:48:27,703
I forgot to add them!

1230
00:48:27,704 --> 00:48:29,104
- What? - Gosh!

1231
00:48:30,204 --> 00:48:31,614
What was I thinking?

1232
00:48:33,674 --> 00:48:35,044
You forgot to add the pine mushrooms.

1233
00:48:35,214 --> 00:48:36,413
I forgot to add the pine mushrooms.

1234
00:48:36,414 --> 00:48:37,483
- What? - The pine mushroom aroma?

1235
00:48:37,484 --> 00:48:39,083
You said it had the pine mushroom aroma.

1236
00:48:39,084 --> 00:48:40,654
It had the pine mushroom aroma.

1237
00:48:40,954 --> 00:48:42,583
You can just dip them in sesame oil.

1238
00:48:42,584 --> 00:48:43,724
(He used pine mushrooms as a diffuser.)

1239
00:48:44,524 --> 00:48:45,954
What was I thinking?

1240
00:48:46,894 --> 00:48:48,593
(He finally has ramyeon with pine mushrooms.)

1241
00:48:48,594 --> 00:48:50,093
- It looks tasty. - I'm going crazy.

1242
00:48:50,094 --> 00:48:52,163
It doesn't get more luxurious than that.

1243
00:48:52,164 --> 00:48:53,334
It looks so tasty.

1244
00:48:53,634 --> 00:48:54,704
Perfect.

1245
00:48:55,964 --> 00:48:57,703
If I don't eat pine mushrooms like that, they'll go bad.

1246
00:48:57,704 --> 00:48:58,733
- That's right. - That's right.

1247
00:48:58,734 --> 00:49:00,604
- It's better to eat them. - I have to eat them like that.

1248
00:49:03,304 --> 00:49:06,974
(He eats ramyeon with wild-harvested pine mushrooms.)

1249
00:49:08,844 --> 00:49:10,414
I made a hot debut as an officiant.

1250
00:49:10,914 --> 00:49:12,114
As for officiating...

1251
00:49:12,714 --> 00:49:14,053
I want to officiate my own wedding.

1252
00:49:14,054 --> 00:49:15,124
- What? - What?

1253
00:49:16,224 --> 00:49:18,624
Moomoo, you haven't gotten married once.

1254
00:49:19,054 --> 00:49:21,094
You aren't even divorced. You haven't gotten married once.

1255
00:49:22,324 --> 00:49:23,624
Just like running,

1256
00:49:23,894 --> 00:49:27,093
you're good at officiating,

1257
00:49:27,094 --> 00:49:28,304
Moomoo.

1258
00:49:31,504 --> 00:49:33,404
(I'm proud of myself.)

1259
00:49:35,144 --> 00:49:36,544
I don't know what I'm feeling.

1260
00:49:37,574 --> 00:49:39,273
You should add "Moo" to officiating.

1261
00:49:39,274 --> 00:49:40,983
- Call it Mooficiating. - Mooficiating.

1262
00:49:40,984 --> 00:49:42,484
- Moomoo's Mooficiating. - Yes.

1263
00:49:43,814 --> 00:49:44,883
I'm hungry.

1264
00:49:44,884 --> 00:49:46,584
- Ramyeon. - Ramyeon is the best.

1265
00:49:49,484 --> 00:49:51,653
- I'll eat ramyeon today. - It's the tastiest.

1266
00:49:51,654 --> 00:49:52,994
I'm craving ramyeon.

1267
00:49:54,564 --> 00:49:57,863
(Ask Moomoo to Mooficiate Your Wedding)

1268
00:49:57,864 --> 00:50:00,064
If I get married, I'll ask Kian84 to host it.

1269
00:50:00,534 --> 00:50:01,603
It was nice.

1270
00:50:01,604 --> 00:50:03,703
It was much better than my cliched way of doing it.

1271
00:50:03,704 --> 00:50:05,173
- It was unpredictable. - Right?

1272
00:50:05,174 --> 00:50:06,474
He didn't cross the line.

1273
00:50:06,674 --> 00:50:07,843
- It was unpredictable. - It was so nice.

1274
00:50:07,844 --> 00:50:09,274
- Well... - Right? He didn't cross the line.

1275
00:50:09,574 --> 00:50:11,573
I'll be able to do it, right?

1276
00:50:11,574 --> 00:50:12,913
- Why do you say that? - Gosh.

1277
00:50:12,914 --> 00:50:14,144
- Don't be like that. - I'll be able to host it, right?

1278
00:50:14,584 --> 00:50:16,343
- Well... - Don't be too realistic.

1279
00:50:16,344 --> 00:50:18,584
Will you say yes if someone asks you to officiate their wedding?

1280
00:50:18,984 --> 00:50:20,514
People who like being bored...

1281
00:50:22,124 --> 00:50:24,224
People who like being bored can ask me anytime.

1282
00:50:24,654 --> 00:50:26,354
(Clicking)

1283
00:50:26,654 --> 00:50:27,864
What?

1284
00:50:29,564 --> 00:50:31,633
- What? - That startled me.

1285
00:50:31,634 --> 00:50:32,694
What?

1286
00:50:33,294 --> 00:50:35,234
(Their eyes go wide.)

1287
00:50:35,904 --> 00:50:37,104
(Opening)

1288
00:50:37,934 --> 00:50:40,674
(Jang Woo makes a surprise visit.)

1289
00:50:41,888 --> 00:50:43,288
- That startled me. - What? This is so sudden.

1290
00:50:44,458 --> 00:50:45,458
Welcome.

1291
00:50:45,688 --> 00:50:47,558
- Congratulations again, new groom. - Thank you.

1292
00:50:47,898 --> 00:50:48,898
Congratulations.

1293
00:50:48,899 --> 00:50:50,057
I'm happy to see him.

1294
00:50:50,058 --> 00:50:52,368
- I felt bad that I couldn't go. - That's right. KEY couldn't come.

1295
00:50:52,728 --> 00:50:54,637
I felt the need to say my last goodbye.

1296
00:50:54,638 --> 00:50:56,368
- My goodness. - My goodness.

1297
00:50:56,698 --> 00:50:59,467
This is your last day...

1298
00:50:59,468 --> 00:51:01,778
- on I Live Alone. - Yes.

1299
00:51:02,078 --> 00:51:04,408
- Please say what you want. - It feels strange.

1300
00:51:05,478 --> 00:51:06,607
I spent my 30s...

1301
00:51:06,608 --> 00:51:07,647
(Looking back)

1302
00:51:07,648 --> 00:51:09,548
with the Rainbow Club members.

1303
00:51:10,678 --> 00:51:13,948
I lived the days anyone would be jealous of.

1304
00:51:14,888 --> 00:51:16,718
- Welcome. - Hello.

1305
00:51:17,058 --> 00:51:20,457
(From 2019,)

1306
00:51:20,458 --> 00:51:23,628
(he spent 6 years...)

1307
00:51:25,828 --> 00:51:30,598
(on I Live Alone.)

1308
00:51:32,208 --> 00:51:37,978
(A rainbow rose in his 30s thanks to that.)

1309
00:51:39,678 --> 00:51:41,418
It still doesn't feel real.

1310
00:51:41,778 --> 00:51:45,718
So I don't know how to define this feeling.

1311
00:51:48,218 --> 00:51:50,988
Thank you so much...

1312
00:51:51,528 --> 00:51:54,427
for being by my side during my 30s and filling my days with happiness.

1313
00:51:54,428 --> 00:51:57,398
I'll live well for anyone to see.

1314
00:51:57,558 --> 00:51:58,827
- Okay. - Thank you.

1315
00:51:58,828 --> 00:51:59,868
My goodness.

1316
00:52:00,298 --> 00:52:01,337
You spent your 30s here,

1317
00:52:01,338 --> 00:52:02,638
but I spent my 30s and 40s here.

1318
00:52:04,338 --> 00:52:06,907
So there will be someone when you go home, right?

1319
00:52:06,908 --> 00:52:08,578
- How does that feel? - My house is very warm.

1320
00:52:09,878 --> 00:52:11,707
- There's warmth. - My goodness.

1321
00:52:11,708 --> 00:52:15,047
No matter how much we turn on the heater, the house remains cold.

1322
00:52:15,048 --> 00:52:16,117
You know what I mean, right?

1323
00:52:16,118 --> 00:52:17,887
There's only so much a heater can do.

1324
00:52:17,888 --> 00:52:19,887
A person's warmth heats up the house differently.

1325
00:52:19,888 --> 00:52:20,948
That's right.

1326
00:52:21,488 --> 00:52:24,318
I actually knew about Jang Woo's visit.

1327
00:52:24,558 --> 00:52:27,457
I prepared something as the president of Rainbow Club.

1328
00:52:27,458 --> 00:52:29,128
- What? - I'll bring it.

1329
00:52:29,228 --> 00:52:30,427
- You should've... - Kian84, help me.

1330
00:52:30,428 --> 00:52:32,768
- discussed it with us. - No.

1331
00:52:32,998 --> 00:52:34,228
- What is it? - What?

1332
00:52:37,068 --> 00:52:39,438
- What? My goodness. - What is it?

1333
00:52:39,768 --> 00:52:41,177
- What? - What is it?

1334
00:52:41,178 --> 00:52:43,578
Firstly, there's an item in Jangwoo Green.

1335
00:52:45,178 --> 00:52:46,678
On top of Jangwoo Green,

1336
00:52:47,308 --> 00:52:48,617
I'll put an appreciation plaque.

1337
00:52:48,618 --> 00:52:50,617
(Here's an appreciation plaque.)

1338
00:52:50,618 --> 00:52:51,948
Please hold it.

1339
00:52:52,148 --> 00:52:54,348
- It's so pretty. - It contains our love.

1340
00:52:55,958 --> 00:52:57,658
"Appreciation plaque for Club Member Lee Jang Woo."

1341
00:52:58,088 --> 00:53:00,188
"As he has appeared on the show as Chef Powder in 2019..."

1342
00:53:00,458 --> 00:53:02,557
"and filled up many people's Friday..."

1343
00:53:02,558 --> 00:53:04,427
"with a lot of energy,"

1344
00:53:04,428 --> 00:53:05,668
"we give this plaque."

1345
00:53:05,968 --> 00:53:07,768
"From the Rainbow Club members of I Live Alone."

1346
00:53:08,268 --> 00:53:10,437
- There's one more thing. - How cute!

1347
00:53:10,438 --> 00:53:13,308
- Congratulations on your wedding - Congratulations on your wedding

1348
00:53:14,608 --> 00:53:16,278
(He opens a banner.)

1349
00:53:16,708 --> 00:53:19,648
- Congratulations, dear Jang Woo - Congratulations, dear Jang Woo

1350
00:53:20,478 --> 00:53:21,947
(Congratulations on your wedding.)

1351
00:53:21,948 --> 00:53:23,247
Congratulations on your wedding.

1352
00:53:23,248 --> 00:53:24,417
Thank you so much.

1353
00:53:24,418 --> 00:53:26,448
Live well. Pay back by living well.

1354
00:53:26,818 --> 00:53:28,558
- We'll live well. - Okay.

1355
00:53:28,818 --> 00:53:30,317
- Thank you. - Congratulations.

1356
00:53:30,318 --> 00:53:32,258
- Congratulations. - It's nice.

1357
00:53:32,728 --> 00:53:33,887
Dowoon,

1358
00:53:33,888 --> 00:53:36,428
- it has been a while. - It has been a while.

1359
00:53:36,628 --> 00:53:37,927
What have you been up to?

1360
00:53:37,928 --> 00:53:39,667
I've practiced...

1361
00:53:39,668 --> 00:53:42,267
- and prepared for a concert. - You've fished as well.

1362
00:53:42,268 --> 00:53:44,337
You were my seafood brother.

1363
00:53:44,338 --> 00:53:45,737
- That's right. - You promised to call...

1364
00:53:45,738 --> 00:53:47,538
if you caught anything.

1365
00:53:48,938 --> 00:53:51,178
(Dowoon had so much love for fishing.)

1366
00:53:52,308 --> 00:53:54,348
(Leave seafood to me.)

1367
00:53:55,018 --> 00:53:58,218
(Seafood brother promised to share sashimi.)

1368
00:53:58,488 --> 00:54:00,488
Haven't you caught anything?

1369
00:54:00,648 --> 00:54:02,758
I haven't caught anything yet.

1370
00:54:02,888 --> 00:54:04,927
If you send me a direct message...

1371
00:54:04,928 --> 00:54:06,228
- A direct message? - A direct message?

1372
00:54:06,788 --> 00:54:10,098
The sea is full of tasty fishes these days.

1373
00:54:10,258 --> 00:54:11,428
Yellowtail is in season.

1374
00:54:11,928 --> 00:54:14,197
- Yellowtail. - Yellowtail tastes good now.

1375
00:54:14,198 --> 00:54:15,767
- I'll need to go on a trip. - You're my seafood brother.

1376
00:54:15,768 --> 00:54:16,798
Okay.

1377
00:54:18,138 --> 00:54:21,208
(Rest for a Beat)

1378
00:54:23,638 --> 00:54:26,108
(Dowoon is holding something.)

1379
00:54:29,018 --> 00:54:30,978
The house looks different during the day.

1380
00:54:31,448 --> 00:54:33,087
I think the house became a bit brighter.

1381
00:54:33,088 --> 00:54:34,447
- It's because it's during the day. - It's because it's during the day.

1382
00:54:34,448 --> 00:54:35,918
- It's because it's during the day. - We're used to seeing it at night.

1383
00:54:37,928 --> 00:54:41,088
(The kitchen looks brighter during the day.)

1384
00:54:44,058 --> 00:54:45,568
His fridge looks the same.

1385
00:54:47,428 --> 00:54:50,238
- Wasn't it there back then? Cake. - Cake.

1386
00:54:51,508 --> 00:54:53,607
(Inside the empty fridge...)

1387
00:54:53,608 --> 00:54:54,678
What?

1388
00:54:55,178 --> 00:54:56,978
(was a cake.)

1389
00:54:59,148 --> 00:55:01,547
(He couldn't fill the fridge due to his busy schedule.)

1390
00:55:01,548 --> 00:55:04,788
It's a cake my bandmate has made for me.

1391
00:55:05,088 --> 00:55:06,718
He'll complain if I throw it away.

1392
00:55:07,518 --> 00:55:09,158
(He couldn't throw away the cake.)

1393
00:55:09,618 --> 00:55:10,718
He's finally filling up the fridge.

1394
00:55:11,058 --> 00:55:12,458
It's my first time seeing the walls of the fridge.

1395
00:55:12,558 --> 00:55:13,758
(Full)

1396
00:55:14,388 --> 00:55:16,528
I spend more time at home these days.

1397
00:55:16,828 --> 00:55:20,127
I decided to use this opportunity to fill up the fridge...

1398
00:55:20,128 --> 00:55:21,638
and went to a grocery store.

1399
00:55:22,068 --> 00:55:23,897
There are times...

1400
00:55:23,898 --> 00:55:26,638
when I feel a bit hungry and think, "I don't want to order anything."

1401
00:55:27,308 --> 00:55:30,307
At those times, it'll be nice to eat frozen food.

1402
00:55:30,308 --> 00:55:32,008
It'll be a special treat.

1403
00:55:32,348 --> 00:55:34,208
I checked the expiration dates.

1404
00:55:34,508 --> 00:55:36,178
It's so nice that they're good for years.

1405
00:55:42,218 --> 00:55:44,018
It's fun to fill the empty fridge.

1406
00:55:50,128 --> 00:55:52,568
When will my fridge get empty like that?

1407
00:55:55,068 --> 00:55:57,398
(Hesitant)

1408
00:55:58,838 --> 00:56:00,308
(Beeping)

1409
00:56:00,938 --> 00:56:02,478
(Halting)

1410
00:56:04,038 --> 00:56:05,807
(Pressing)

1411
00:56:05,808 --> 00:56:07,848
- He hasn't done it before. - He's using it for the first time.

1412
00:56:09,348 --> 00:56:10,778
It's tricky at first.

1413
00:56:12,648 --> 00:56:14,217
How does it work?

1414
00:56:14,218 --> 00:56:16,288
- It's locked at first. - That's right.

1415
00:56:16,958 --> 00:56:19,458
I used the induction stove for the first time since moving.

1416
00:56:19,788 --> 00:56:22,458
My schedule was packed. I was so busy.

1417
00:56:23,128 --> 00:56:24,698
I was using it after six months.

1418
00:56:24,998 --> 00:56:26,528
It's tricky.

1419
00:56:28,468 --> 00:56:30,738
He must've been busy. There's nothing at home.

1420
00:56:33,468 --> 00:56:36,678
(He takes out hot sauce and frozen pork belly slices.)

1421
00:56:40,348 --> 00:56:43,048
(He pours frozen pork belly slices to the pan.)

1422
00:56:47,388 --> 00:56:48,888
I know what he's going to make.

1423
00:56:50,118 --> 00:56:51,658
- What's that? - What's that?

1424
00:56:51,888 --> 00:56:53,727
- It's a typical dish... - I recognize it.

1425
00:56:53,728 --> 00:56:55,428
for people who can't cook.

1426
00:56:55,698 --> 00:56:57,357
You know the hotel breakfast, right?

1427
00:56:57,358 --> 00:56:59,668
- That's right. - That's what it is, right?

1428
00:56:59,898 --> 00:57:01,127
- What? - The hotel breakfast?

1429
00:57:01,128 --> 00:57:02,737
- American breakfast. - What?

1430
00:57:02,738 --> 00:57:04,297
American breakfast?

1431
00:57:04,298 --> 00:57:06,737
To make that, you should've cooked...

1432
00:57:06,738 --> 00:57:08,877
- the pork belly slices 1st. - You should've done that 1st.

1433
00:57:08,878 --> 00:57:09,977
- That's right. - Otherwise,

1434
00:57:09,978 --> 00:57:12,447
the gamey smell goes to the egg.

1435
00:57:12,448 --> 00:57:14,677
If he were someone on that level who could make such calculations,

1436
00:57:14,678 --> 00:57:16,048
- he wouldn't have made that. - No.

1437
00:57:16,948 --> 00:57:18,947
It's the first time I recognized a dish before anyone.

1438
00:57:18,948 --> 00:57:21,418
- Thank you so much. - I had no idea what it was.

1439
00:57:24,828 --> 00:57:27,688
(He makes something like a hotel breakfast.)

1440
00:57:28,358 --> 00:57:29,658
The first meal of the day is...

1441
00:57:30,158 --> 00:57:32,968
something I ate often in my previous house.

1442
00:57:33,268 --> 00:57:34,968
- It's the K-American breakfast. - That's right.

1443
00:57:36,498 --> 00:57:40,508
Pork belly slices and pseudo-scrambled eggs.

1444
00:57:44,038 --> 00:57:46,007
A lot of eggshell pieces must've gone in.

1445
00:57:46,008 --> 00:57:47,077
That's right.

1446
00:57:47,078 --> 00:57:48,318
(Picking out)

1447
00:57:50,448 --> 00:57:51,547
(Laughing)

1448
00:57:51,548 --> 00:57:52,588
He's laughing.

1449
00:57:59,628 --> 00:58:00,958
Seriously, what's this?

1450
00:58:03,458 --> 00:58:05,968
- This isn't what I imagined. - I'm a bit better at cooking.

1451
00:58:08,768 --> 00:58:12,638
It didn't look as appetizing as I had imagined.

1452
00:58:13,908 --> 00:58:17,238
(K-American breakfast is different than what he imagined.)

1453
00:58:18,648 --> 00:58:19,808
Gosh, it's hot!

1454
00:58:20,478 --> 00:58:22,347
- Gosh, it's hot! - The heat is too high.

1455
00:58:22,348 --> 00:58:23,478
Lower the heat.

1456
00:58:25,948 --> 00:58:29,988
(Smoke fills up the house, but he remains calm.)

1457
00:58:30,788 --> 00:58:32,257
I like burning my food a bit.

1458
00:58:32,258 --> 00:58:33,588
It looks like something I'd like.

1459
00:58:34,128 --> 00:58:36,098
- I'll make it for you next time. - It's okay.

1460
00:58:37,698 --> 00:58:39,628
In most cases, Kian84 doesn't say no.

1461
00:58:39,998 --> 00:58:41,697
Kian84 makes something like that and freezes it.

1462
00:58:41,698 --> 00:58:42,698
- That's right. - What?

1463
00:58:42,699 --> 00:58:44,297
He freezes warm meals...

1464
00:58:44,298 --> 00:58:45,507
and eats frozen food.

1465
00:58:45,508 --> 00:58:47,467
He's Elsa of the cooking scene.

1466
00:58:47,468 --> 00:58:49,207
That's why I don't need to buy frozen food.

1467
00:58:49,208 --> 00:58:51,038
I buy fresh food and freeze it.

1468
00:58:53,008 --> 00:58:54,548
(Smoky)

1469
00:58:54,948 --> 00:58:56,918
(Carefree)

1470
00:58:59,018 --> 00:59:00,118
Oh, my.

1471
00:59:04,018 --> 00:59:05,488
(My goodness)

1472
00:59:05,688 --> 00:59:07,388
He should've turned on the light from the start.

1473
00:59:07,888 --> 00:59:09,098
He hadn't turned it on before.

1474
00:59:09,198 --> 00:59:10,198
(I see.)

1475
00:59:10,199 --> 00:59:11,258
That's right.

1476
00:59:11,468 --> 00:59:13,567
- It looks appetizing, right? - It looks nice.

1477
00:59:13,568 --> 00:59:15,097
- It looks pretty nice. - Yes.

1478
00:59:15,098 --> 00:59:16,098
- As it burned. - Right.

1479
00:59:17,198 --> 00:59:19,308
(This should be enough.)

1480
00:59:20,108 --> 00:59:22,578
I think it will be okay with some salt and pepper.

1481
00:59:27,048 --> 00:59:29,118
(He takes out the instant rice that he heated up.)

1482
00:59:30,018 --> 00:59:31,118
Look at how dirty it became.

1483
00:59:32,048 --> 00:59:33,217
That's why I don't cook.

1484
00:59:33,218 --> 00:59:34,348
Same here.

1485
00:59:40,028 --> 00:59:41,257
(He shakes out the hot sauce as well.)

1486
00:59:41,258 --> 00:59:42,258
You're going to have it with some hot sauce?

1487
00:59:42,259 --> 00:59:43,298
Yes.

1488
00:59:43,858 --> 00:59:45,668
He bought it because he was American.

1489
00:59:46,128 --> 00:59:47,198
K-American.

1490
00:59:49,038 --> 00:59:51,038
(All right.)

1491
00:59:52,638 --> 00:59:55,678
(Let's try my K-American breakfast.)

1492
00:59:58,278 --> 00:59:59,677
- Gosh, that's hot! - What's wrong?

1493
00:59:59,678 --> 01:00:00,807
- You grabbed the hot part, right? - Be careful.

1494
01:00:00,808 --> 01:00:01,918
- Right. - Gosh!

1495
01:00:02,218 --> 01:00:03,277
What?

1496
01:00:03,278 --> 01:00:04,748
(He grabbed the part that was heated up.)

1497
01:00:06,518 --> 01:00:07,917
I grabbed it without putting any thought to it.

1498
01:00:07,918 --> 01:00:09,187
You're showing all of your charms.

1499
01:00:09,188 --> 01:00:10,188
Is that so?

1500
01:00:10,189 --> 01:00:12,157
- He has a lot of clumsy charms. - What a man.

1501
01:00:12,158 --> 01:00:13,188
Nice.

1502
01:00:14,428 --> 01:00:16,058
(Let's calm down.)

1503
01:00:17,298 --> 01:00:19,628
(He picks up a lot of the meat first.)

1504
01:00:20,068 --> 01:00:21,738
(He dips it in the hot sauce.)

1505
01:00:22,798 --> 01:00:24,698
(Eating it in one bite)

1506
01:00:26,738 --> 01:00:27,868
These things taste good.

1507
01:00:29,138 --> 01:00:30,708
I'm sure that's not what's good.

1508
01:00:30,908 --> 01:00:32,208
His standards of taste are low.

1509
01:00:32,578 --> 01:00:34,708
- What are you trying to say? - For example, you two...

1510
01:00:34,878 --> 01:00:36,718
have experienced many different kinds of good food.

1511
01:00:37,648 --> 01:00:39,887
- But as for him... - No. I like good food as well.

1512
01:00:39,888 --> 01:00:41,048
To be honest,

1513
01:00:41,988 --> 01:00:43,457
it's hard to enjoy...

1514
01:00:43,458 --> 01:00:45,327
really good food at home.

1515
01:00:45,328 --> 01:00:46,528
It looks awful.

1516
01:00:48,858 --> 01:00:49,958
You two failed to connect this time.

1517
01:00:52,568 --> 01:00:54,398
(He suddenly gets up.)

1518
01:01:02,438 --> 01:01:03,638
That's a really nice cup.

1519
01:01:04,038 --> 01:01:06,307
- Why is it nice? - It's huge. I can drink all day.

1520
01:01:06,308 --> 01:01:07,848
- It's definitely big. - It's huge!

1521
01:01:08,578 --> 01:01:10,247
What is that? Isn't that a beer glass?

1522
01:01:10,248 --> 01:01:11,248
Gosh, it's big.

1523
01:01:15,788 --> 01:01:17,858
It was good though. I enjoyed it.

1524
01:01:18,528 --> 01:01:19,958
It was just like...

1525
01:01:20,658 --> 01:01:22,528
a K-American breakfast.

1526
01:01:24,498 --> 01:01:25,567
It was salty...

1527
01:01:25,568 --> 01:01:26,628
(It was decently salty...)

1528
01:01:27,498 --> 01:01:28,498
and spicy.

1529
01:01:28,498 --> 01:01:29,498
(and spicy.)

1530
01:01:30,198 --> 01:01:31,238
It tasted just right.

1531
01:01:31,498 --> 01:01:32,508
I enjoyed it.

1532
01:01:32,638 --> 01:01:33,808
That's all that matters.

1533
01:01:35,238 --> 01:01:37,038
You're eating it well.

1534
01:01:39,278 --> 01:01:42,078
There's not a single tuna can or dried laver packet for a side dish.

1535
01:01:43,148 --> 01:01:45,448
(Empty)

1536
01:01:45,548 --> 01:01:47,047
It means he was satisfied with what he had.

1537
01:01:47,048 --> 01:01:49,388
Right. I just eat what I have.

1538
01:01:49,718 --> 01:01:52,228
(He scrapes the leftover rice.)

1539
01:01:53,388 --> 01:01:55,958
(Clear)

1540
01:01:57,258 --> 01:01:59,197
- Gosh, I like that. - That's neat.

1541
01:01:59,198 --> 01:02:00,428
- Clearing up everything. - Right.

1542
01:02:01,168 --> 01:02:03,398
(Picking up)

1543
01:02:06,708 --> 01:02:09,908
(The used dishes head straight to the sink.)

1544
01:02:11,308 --> 01:02:13,008
(Wiping)

1545
01:02:15,178 --> 01:02:16,217
(He wipes the table right away as well.)

1546
01:02:16,218 --> 01:02:17,518
(Scrubbing hard)

1547
01:02:17,718 --> 01:02:19,087
It's because the house is white all around.

1548
01:02:19,088 --> 01:02:21,118
- Right. - Right.

1549
01:02:21,988 --> 01:02:24,358
(He cleans the induction stove neatly as well.)

1550
01:02:25,228 --> 01:02:26,788
(Picking up)

1551
01:02:27,258 --> 01:02:29,328
(I will make sure to clean up everything.)

1552
01:02:32,568 --> 01:02:33,728
Well,

1553
01:02:34,568 --> 01:02:36,668
I'm not that much of a neat freak,

1554
01:02:37,138 --> 01:02:39,368
but I remember what my house originally looked like.

1555
01:02:41,308 --> 01:02:43,307
As the house was originally neat,

1556
01:02:43,308 --> 01:02:46,077
I want to keep it that way even after eating.

1557
01:02:46,078 --> 01:02:49,218
(He cleans up right away.)

1558
01:02:49,478 --> 01:02:51,888
(Wiping)

1559
01:02:53,288 --> 01:02:55,358
I got rid of the grease.

1560
01:02:56,818 --> 01:02:59,558
(He washes the dishes next.)

1561
01:03:00,658 --> 01:03:01,957
(Tapping)

1562
01:03:01,958 --> 01:03:03,028
What's going on?

1563
01:03:03,428 --> 01:03:06,298
(He cleans up the food waste neatly as well.)

1564
01:03:09,838 --> 01:03:10,838
(Spraying)

1565
01:03:10,838 --> 01:03:11,838
What's that?

1566
01:03:12,538 --> 01:03:14,007
(He even cleans the sink with the handwash.)

1567
01:03:14,008 --> 01:03:16,008
- I do that once in a while. - Once in a while?

1568
01:03:18,308 --> 01:03:20,278
- Gosh, I can't believe him. - He's so neat.

1569
01:03:22,248 --> 01:03:24,478
Leaving that place dirty attracts roaches, doesn't it?

1570
01:03:24,618 --> 01:03:26,548
That area usually doesn't get roaches.

1571
01:03:28,088 --> 01:03:30,388
(He finishes up.)

1572
01:03:32,028 --> 01:03:34,288
(Gulping)

1573
01:03:35,228 --> 01:03:36,228
(Feeling refreshed)

1574
01:03:38,968 --> 01:03:41,598
(The kitchen has been restored.)

1575
01:03:43,268 --> 01:03:44,467
That's neat.

1576
01:03:44,468 --> 01:03:45,468
(Neat)

1577
01:03:46,268 --> 01:03:49,138
(He picks up his bag.)

1578
01:03:50,008 --> 01:03:52,848
(Walking out)

1579
01:03:53,808 --> 01:03:57,178
(Dowoon heads out.)

1580
01:03:59,388 --> 01:04:00,548
Where are you going?

1581
01:04:06,228 --> 01:04:08,528
(Whistling)

1582
01:04:10,158 --> 01:04:11,358
The weather is great too.

1583
01:04:13,368 --> 01:04:15,567
(He enters a building.)

1584
01:04:15,568 --> 01:04:17,068
It must be the gym or his original home.

1585
01:04:17,538 --> 01:04:18,608
Original home?

1586
01:04:21,038 --> 01:04:24,378
(He enters an unknown place.)

1587
01:04:26,178 --> 01:04:29,848
(He walks in naturally.)

1588
01:04:30,978 --> 01:04:32,218
- What? - What?

1589
01:04:36,918 --> 01:04:37,988
The drummer.

1590
01:04:38,988 --> 01:04:41,287
It's the band master's home.

1591
01:04:41,288 --> 01:04:43,927
It's also my practice room and studio.

1592
01:04:43,928 --> 01:04:46,068
I was told that there was an empty room,

1593
01:04:46,168 --> 01:04:48,668
so I asked to use it while paying for the monthly rent.

1594
01:04:49,068 --> 01:04:50,138
He said that was fine.

1595
01:04:50,268 --> 01:04:52,508
I've been gratefully using it up until now.

1596
01:04:53,338 --> 01:04:54,438
It's set up nicely.

1597
01:04:54,838 --> 01:04:57,478
There are times when recordings are done there.

1598
01:04:57,738 --> 01:04:59,847
Other people use it as well.

1599
01:04:59,848 --> 01:05:00,848
(There are other rooms as well.)

1600
01:05:01,878 --> 01:05:04,018
- Do you go often? - Yes. I go really often.

1601
01:05:04,248 --> 01:05:05,788
Around 4 to 5 times a week.

1602
01:05:08,048 --> 01:05:11,218
(He drops his bag naturally.)

1603
01:05:11,658 --> 01:05:13,058
(Shall I start practicing?)

1604
01:05:14,288 --> 01:05:16,398
Drummers are so impressive.

1605
01:05:18,768 --> 01:05:20,527
How do you play with slippers on?

1606
01:05:20,528 --> 01:05:23,338
I love those slippers.

1607
01:05:23,668 --> 01:05:26,138
The sole is really thin.

1608
01:05:26,438 --> 01:05:29,378
It's easier to hit the drum pedal with thin soles.

1609
01:05:29,538 --> 01:05:30,907
Do you need to feel the pedal more?

1610
01:05:30,908 --> 01:05:31,978
Do they not come off while playing?

1611
01:05:32,078 --> 01:05:33,678
They don't come off easily.

1612
01:05:34,508 --> 01:05:36,478
(He's ready with his favorite slippers.)

1613
01:05:36,818 --> 01:05:37,848
(Reaching out)

1614
01:05:39,388 --> 01:05:40,717
- What's that? - The metronome.

1615
01:05:40,718 --> 01:05:41,817
The metronome?

1616
01:05:41,818 --> 01:05:43,788
- The tempo is kept with that? - Yes.

1617
01:05:45,258 --> 01:05:47,458
(Shall I begin?)

1618
01:05:48,598 --> 01:05:50,828
(Splitting up the beat with the metronome)

1619
01:05:52,728 --> 01:05:54,228
- Gosh! - Gosh!

1620
01:05:59,268 --> 01:06:01,567
- That must be the basics. - Right.

1621
01:06:01,568 --> 01:06:02,808
That must be done as a warmup.

1622
01:06:05,908 --> 01:06:06,947
What's going on?

1623
01:06:06,948 --> 01:06:07,978
Who was that?

1624
01:06:08,078 --> 01:06:09,078
What?

1625
01:06:10,078 --> 01:06:11,078
What's going on?

1626
01:06:11,079 --> 01:06:13,087
- Is it his friend? - Is it the owner of the house?

1627
01:06:13,088 --> 01:06:14,147
What?

1628
01:06:14,148 --> 01:06:15,658
(Who is that?)

1629
01:06:16,688 --> 01:06:19,388
(Someone naturally came into the practice room.)

1630
01:06:20,028 --> 01:06:22,128
(Staring)

1631
01:06:22,598 --> 01:06:24,798
- It's high, isn't it? - It's really high.

1632
01:06:24,928 --> 01:06:26,997
It's a completely different sound from when we perform.

1633
01:06:26,998 --> 01:06:29,038
- Right? - It's way too high.

1634
01:06:29,238 --> 01:06:30,667
Let's loosen it up then.

1635
01:06:30,668 --> 01:06:32,167
(The drum suddenly gets loosened up.)

1636
01:06:32,168 --> 01:06:33,168
- What? - Gosh.

1637
01:06:34,138 --> 01:06:36,008
- All right. - How low must it be?

1638
01:06:39,008 --> 01:06:40,048
It's still high.

1639
01:06:40,478 --> 01:06:43,718
The guy who just came in...

1640
01:06:43,948 --> 01:06:46,248
is the drummer of Sister's Barbershop, Jeon Dae Jeong.

1641
01:06:47,488 --> 01:06:49,087
Oh, Sister's Barbershop?

1642
01:06:49,088 --> 01:06:50,688
(Sister's Barbershop made its debut in 1996.)

1643
01:06:51,188 --> 01:06:52,227
(The root of Korea's modern rock)

1644
01:06:52,228 --> 01:06:53,858
He's from our generation.

1645
01:06:54,858 --> 01:06:58,998
He's a senior I rely on a lot and am very grateful for.

1646
01:06:59,168 --> 01:07:00,528
Whenever we hold concerts,

1647
01:07:00,698 --> 01:07:03,568
he helps out with the drums as the technician.

1648
01:07:03,798 --> 01:07:05,037
Drum tuning is really difficult.

1649
01:07:05,038 --> 01:07:06,467
- Drums are tuned as well? - Yes.

1650
01:07:06,468 --> 01:07:08,337
Drum tuning is really complicated.

1651
01:07:08,338 --> 01:07:09,908
- It seems like so. - You'll see why.

1652
01:07:10,008 --> 01:07:11,008
- Really? - That's fascinating.

1653
01:07:11,009 --> 01:07:12,548
I wanted to learn from him.

1654
01:07:12,908 --> 01:07:14,808
I was the host of a band audition.

1655
01:07:14,978 --> 01:07:16,717
People like him came out for each band...

1656
01:07:16,718 --> 01:07:18,417
and kept working on the drums.

1657
01:07:18,418 --> 01:07:19,487
The drums were tightened up and loosened up constantly.

1658
01:07:19,488 --> 01:07:21,388
Right. That's done for 20 to 30 minutes.

1659
01:07:22,218 --> 01:07:26,628
(A technician is essential for the drums for a performance.)

1660
01:07:26,988 --> 01:07:29,097
I've never thought that drums needed to be tuned.

1661
01:07:29,098 --> 01:07:30,157
I thought the drum was completely replaced...

1662
01:07:30,158 --> 01:07:31,158
if it sounded bad.

1663
01:07:31,159 --> 01:07:32,967
- Just as a whole. - No.

1664
01:07:32,968 --> 01:07:35,338
Do all of the drums have to be tuned after a four-hour performance?

1665
01:07:35,538 --> 01:07:37,667
He would come early the next day...

1666
01:07:37,668 --> 01:07:39,667
- and give them a check. - I see.

1667
01:07:39,668 --> 01:07:40,707
Do the drums loosen up after a performance?

1668
01:07:40,708 --> 01:07:42,477
- Yes. They loosen up. - I suppose so.

1669
01:07:42,478 --> 01:07:44,177
They get tuned again for another performance?

1670
01:07:44,178 --> 01:07:45,777
(The technician is essential for performances.)

1671
01:07:45,778 --> 01:07:47,978
- Do they sound different? - Yes.

1672
01:07:48,548 --> 01:07:50,278
It could be because of the skin.

1673
01:07:50,448 --> 01:07:52,648
- The skin is a bit weird. - Right.

1674
01:07:52,788 --> 01:07:54,217
The skin is quite old.

1675
01:07:54,218 --> 01:07:55,858
- What's the skin? - The drum skin.

1676
01:07:55,988 --> 01:07:58,218
- It's the top part of the drum. - Right.

1677
01:07:58,388 --> 01:08:00,427
- Is that one in better condition? - This one is in good condition.

1678
01:08:00,428 --> 01:08:01,887
- Really? - The skin is replaced as well?

1679
01:08:01,888 --> 01:08:02,928
Let's see.

1680
01:08:03,428 --> 01:08:04,597
That's fascinating.

1681
01:08:04,598 --> 01:08:07,098
(Hold on.)

1682
01:08:08,428 --> 01:08:13,338
(It's filled with drum tuning tools.)

1683
01:08:15,208 --> 01:08:19,948
(The tools get taken out in earnest.)

1684
01:08:20,708 --> 01:08:21,778
Here.

1685
01:08:22,818 --> 01:08:24,517
I worked on this in the practice room for four hours.

1686
01:08:24,518 --> 01:08:25,888
- I just couldn't deal with it. - Four hours?

1687
01:08:26,218 --> 01:08:28,888
I tightened it up and loosened it up for four hours.

1688
01:08:29,458 --> 01:08:31,058
I gave up in the end.

1689
01:08:31,458 --> 01:08:33,557
(The bolts are loosened with a special drill.)

1690
01:08:33,558 --> 01:08:34,728
So that's how it's done?

1691
01:08:35,398 --> 01:08:36,797
So that comes off.

1692
01:08:36,798 --> 01:08:37,827
Pass it to me.

1693
01:08:37,828 --> 01:08:38,828
(The old drum skin finally gets removed.)

1694
01:08:39,528 --> 01:08:41,398
The first one that was tuned was the snare.

1695
01:08:41,928 --> 01:08:43,538
It's the one that gets played a lot.

1696
01:08:44,738 --> 01:08:46,637
- The snare. - Drumming is really hard.

1697
01:08:46,638 --> 01:08:47,738
Like that?

1698
01:08:47,838 --> 01:08:49,637
(The new drum skin is placed on the snare.)

1699
01:08:49,638 --> 01:08:50,708
There.

1700
01:08:51,438 --> 01:08:52,477
This goes on top.

1701
01:08:52,478 --> 01:08:55,417
(The loosened up bolts must be tightened again.)

1702
01:08:55,418 --> 01:08:56,918
Screw them on.

1703
01:08:57,348 --> 01:08:59,687
(Using his hands to turn them)

1704
01:08:59,688 --> 01:09:01,618
All the way until the end.

1705
01:09:02,358 --> 01:09:04,088
- Only by hand, right? - Right.

1706
01:09:04,658 --> 01:09:05,988
The one that's being turned...

1707
01:09:06,528 --> 01:09:08,588
- shouldn't be too tight. - Right.

1708
01:09:08,728 --> 01:09:10,127
They should be tightened gently.

1709
01:09:10,128 --> 01:09:12,168
- Right. - They aren't fastened tightly.

1710
01:09:12,468 --> 01:09:14,028
They can't be dealt with recklessly.

1711
01:09:14,128 --> 01:09:15,837
- Just so that they don't turn. - Right.

1712
01:09:15,838 --> 01:09:17,298
- This much. - They don't turn, right?

1713
01:09:17,668 --> 01:09:19,108
They're secured first.

1714
01:09:19,538 --> 01:09:21,837
Other instruments use a tuner to get tuned.

1715
01:09:21,838 --> 01:09:24,408
So that's completely different from tuning other instruments.

1716
01:09:24,438 --> 01:09:27,807
Go around and check if the sounds are the same.

1717
01:09:27,808 --> 01:09:29,348
- Gosh. - Gosh.

1718
01:09:29,548 --> 01:09:31,647
(Tapping)

1719
01:09:31,648 --> 01:09:32,787
- What do you think? - It's higher.

1720
01:09:32,788 --> 01:09:34,617
Right. That's when you lower it.

1721
01:09:34,618 --> 01:09:36,318
Is it this one or this one?

1722
01:09:36,518 --> 01:09:38,018
I'll go with this one.

1723
01:09:38,218 --> 01:09:39,328
So...

1724
01:09:39,658 --> 01:09:41,528
So the balance is important as well.

1725
01:09:43,328 --> 01:09:44,797
- What do you think? - That's fascinating.

1726
01:09:44,798 --> 01:09:45,798
It's nice.

1727
01:09:45,799 --> 01:09:48,027
Gosh, those of us who don't play are clueless.

1728
01:09:48,028 --> 01:09:49,038
It all sounds the same.

1729
01:09:49,938 --> 01:09:51,937
(He checks the snare that has been tuned.)

1730
01:09:51,938 --> 01:09:53,038
It's nice.

1731
01:09:55,268 --> 01:09:57,908
(The tuning is done with his talented hands.)

1732
01:09:59,248 --> 01:10:00,378
Good.

1733
01:10:03,348 --> 01:10:04,417
(The pitch has been brought down after getting tuned.)

1734
01:10:04,418 --> 01:10:06,047
- Right. - Right.

1735
01:10:06,048 --> 01:10:07,617
Give it a try and loosen this one up as well.

1736
01:10:07,618 --> 01:10:09,218
- Should I try with this one? - Sure.

1737
01:10:10,018 --> 01:10:11,488
This one is really hard to tune.

1738
01:10:11,958 --> 01:10:16,157
He can't stay by my side forever.

1739
01:10:16,158 --> 01:10:17,757
I should learn how to tune the drums myself...

1740
01:10:17,758 --> 01:10:20,028
as I might happen to perform on my own.

1741
01:10:20,528 --> 01:10:22,897
I heard there was a specific sound of each drum.

1742
01:10:22,898 --> 01:10:24,168
- Right. - Right.

1743
01:10:25,608 --> 01:10:27,408
(Burning with passion)

1744
01:10:27,838 --> 01:10:30,337
I'll try to remember the exact sound...

1745
01:10:30,338 --> 01:10:31,508
and tune it just the same way.

1746
01:10:32,678 --> 01:10:33,747
- Really? - Yes.

1747
01:10:33,748 --> 01:10:35,277
It's a special private lesson.

1748
01:10:35,278 --> 01:10:37,118
Right. This is really hard.

1749
01:10:37,148 --> 01:10:38,848
This is something you can't learn elsewhere.

1750
01:10:40,188 --> 01:10:43,388
(He confidently loosens up the bolts first.)

1751
01:10:44,188 --> 01:10:45,218
All right.

1752
01:10:47,328 --> 01:10:49,328
(Reaching out)

1753
01:10:49,728 --> 01:10:52,528
- Don't touch it. It's mine. - Oh, is that so?

1754
01:10:53,928 --> 01:10:56,438
(I'll do it on my own.)

1755
01:10:59,938 --> 01:11:02,138
Shall I begin?

1756
01:11:02,508 --> 01:11:04,238
It will definitely sound dry right now.

1757
01:11:04,878 --> 01:11:06,777
- That's the first step done. - Be bold.

1758
01:11:06,778 --> 01:11:08,477
Be bold. Don't back down.

1759
01:11:08,478 --> 01:11:09,848
- Screw it tight. - Right.

1760
01:11:10,618 --> 01:11:12,077
This feels like a prison escape.

1761
01:11:12,078 --> 01:11:13,448
(Serious)

1762
01:11:13,888 --> 01:11:16,558
(He screws the bolt boldly.)

1763
01:11:17,488 --> 01:11:20,387
(Ringing)

1764
01:11:20,388 --> 01:11:21,428
Gosh.

1765
01:11:21,558 --> 01:11:23,597
- There's a bit of ringing. - It sounds low.

1766
01:11:23,598 --> 01:11:24,727
It sounds off.

1767
01:11:24,728 --> 01:11:25,798
(Ringing)

1768
01:11:26,528 --> 01:11:27,528
(Clear)

1769
01:11:27,928 --> 01:11:28,938
(Ringing)

1770
01:11:29,438 --> 01:11:31,037
It does sound strange even to us who don't know much.

1771
01:11:31,038 --> 01:11:32,767
- It's a bit different. - It sounds off even to us.

1772
01:11:32,768 --> 01:11:33,967
It doesn't sound pretty when you hear it.

1773
01:11:33,968 --> 01:11:35,238
- It doesn't sound pretty. - No.

1774
01:11:36,738 --> 01:11:38,178
(Crying)

1775
01:11:39,578 --> 01:11:40,678
I should loosen it up again.

1776
01:11:40,708 --> 01:11:43,778
You have to do this 10,000 times to get a better sound.

1777
01:11:44,248 --> 01:11:45,248
He has to do it 10,000 times?

1778
01:11:45,518 --> 01:11:48,018
That must be how he trained himself.

1779
01:11:48,148 --> 01:11:49,387
This is really hard.

1780
01:11:49,388 --> 01:11:51,457
So the tuning makes a big difference.

1781
01:11:51,458 --> 01:11:53,928
I think it's even harder because it's done just by ear.

1782
01:11:54,058 --> 01:11:56,428
Try to fix the sound.

1783
01:11:57,228 --> 01:11:58,328
It doesn't sound nice.

1784
01:11:58,528 --> 01:12:00,268
- Right. - Try to fix it.

1785
01:12:00,828 --> 01:12:02,097
What will he do to fix it?

1786
01:12:02,098 --> 01:12:03,737
- Will he get it fixed right away? - I'm curious.

1787
01:12:03,738 --> 01:12:05,138
(Tapping carefully)

1788
01:12:06,208 --> 01:12:07,807
It was ringing up there.

1789
01:12:07,808 --> 01:12:09,238
He's like a human cultural asset.

1790
01:12:10,208 --> 01:12:12,108
So that's how it's done.

1791
01:12:14,248 --> 01:12:16,878
(He tightens the bolts without hesitation.)

1792
01:12:19,248 --> 01:12:20,517
That's why a professional is different.

1793
01:12:20,518 --> 01:12:23,417
He finds the right balance and fixes the lacking parts.

1794
01:12:23,418 --> 01:12:25,218
- Right. - That's how it's done.

1795
01:12:27,328 --> 01:12:28,557
(Amazed)

1796
01:12:28,558 --> 01:12:29,798
That will be too hard to do.

1797
01:12:32,228 --> 01:12:33,468
I think I'm done with it.

1798
01:12:33,798 --> 01:12:35,598
(Clear)

1799
01:12:36,238 --> 01:12:38,168
- It sounds different. - It sounds different.

1800
01:12:38,298 --> 01:12:39,338
I think it sounds nice.

1801
01:12:39,608 --> 01:12:41,568
- Gosh, that's fascinating. - Gosh.

1802
01:12:41,738 --> 01:12:43,307
(Let's work on the other drums.)

1803
01:12:43,308 --> 01:12:45,237
I think it will take a lot of time...

1804
01:12:45,238 --> 01:12:47,078
for me to do it on my own.

1805
01:12:47,248 --> 01:12:48,578
That sounds nice.

1806
